    "score": 8.844344538989954,
    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Is it possible to be a professional orchestral musician as a late starter?\nauthor: Lydia Tay\ndate: 2021-02-26\n---\nMost threads on late starts assume the age of starting to be 12-15. But what about those who start in their 20s?\n\nI began playing the violin at 17 as a hobby, but I've since switched to the viola (I know, the heresy!) and I'm now 25 years old.\n\nIs it possible at all to become an orchestral musician in a prominent or at least established orchestra like the Singapore Symphony Orchestra as a very late starter? Are there any examples of musicians who have done that?\n\nTweet\n\nin a way I think the question is framed in a misleading way. A late starter can achieve very standards that are in may ways commensurate with the level of some professional orchestras. But Honestly speaking, the standards in the top tier are so high that unless you are someone who can stand up on two days notice and give a decent rendition of a major concerto you just aren't there. The Berlin Phil is just a bunch of geniuses who couldnT all get booked for the Tchaikovsky concerto.\n\nWhat I would rather note is irrespective of how well you play at an audition you will be up against huge numbers of players who also play at a fantastic level. They have been training to be soloists and then become orchestral players for many years at the finest institutes. BUt the fundamental difference is that th:e.y have, by default, a lot more experience with the orchestral repertoire and active concertizing. It is fairly certain that at this point you are just too far behind in this regard. So, faced with two excellent players, one of whom knows the repertoire the choice of who is going to be employed is obvious .\n\nThe second point is that we have entered an era in which orchestral playing as a job is being systematically strangled. Professionals all over the world are quitting simply because they cannot feed their families in a lockdown. I am not saying the music world is dying exactly but, like many other areas of human endeavour, it is going through a radical restructuring and , just my opinion, it is rather hard to predict who will be doing what, where and in what form.\n\nOne final point, many many years ago the then director of the BBC spoke at my graduation from Music College. His words were quite clear 'If you have any doubts at all, then don't become a professional"
  }
