    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Alex Azar - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases International ISNI VIAF FAST WorldCat National Germany United States\ndate: 2005-12-17\n---\nname: Alex Azar\noffice: 24th United States Secretary of Health and Human Services\npresident: Donald Trump\ndeputy: Eric Hargan\nterm_start: January 29, 2018\nterm_end: January 20, 2021\npredecessor: Tom Price\nsuccessor: Xavier Becerra\noffice1: 1st Chairman of the White House Coronavirus Task Force\npresident1: Donald Trump\nterm_start1: January 29, 2020\nterm_end1: February 26, 2020\npredecessor1: Office established\nsuccessor1: Mike Pence\noffice2: 20th United States Deputy Secretary of Health and Human Services\npresident2: George W. Bush\nterm_start2: January 22, 2005\nterm_end2: February 4, 2007\npredecessor2: Claude Allen\nsuccessor2: Tevi Troy\noffice3: General Counsel of the United States Department of Health and Human Services\npresident3: George W. Bush\nterm_start3: August 8, 2001\nterm_end3: January 22, 2005\npredecessor3: Harriet S. Rabb\nsuccessor3: Daniel Meron\nbirth_name: Alex Michael Azar II\nbirth_date: 17 June 1967\nbirth_place: Johnstown, Pennsylvania, U.S.\neducation: Dartmouth College (BA)Yale University (JD)\nparty: Republican\nchildren: 2\n\nAlex Michael Azar II (; born June 17, 1967) is an American attorney, businessman, lobbyist, and former pharmaceutical executive who served as the 24th U.S. secretary of health and human services from 2018 to 2021. He was also chairman of the White House Coronavirus Task Force from its inception in January 2020 to February 2020, when he was replaced by Vice President Mike Pence.\n\nIn 2021, he was appointed a senior executive in residence at the Miami Herbert Business School at the University of Miami.\n\nFrom 2012 to 2017, Azar was president of the U.S. division of Eli Lilly and Company, a major drug company, and a member of the board of directors of the Biotechnology Innovation"
